          %h2#faq FAQ
          %h3#WhatIsStringTheory What is string theory?
          %p
            What is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            is a variant of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbation theory
            in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (QFT). It is a definition of an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"}> S-matrix
            ” of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            of quantum objects which is similar to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            obtained in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            One way that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is defined in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+field+theory"} perturbative field theory
            is – in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            – as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} summing
            over labelled
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphs"} graphs
            (called
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            of a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            defined on these graphs (the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"} virtual particles
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} scattering amplitude
            are to be computed). As a (natural) variant of this, the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            is defined to be a similar series, but instead of over 1-dimensional graphs now over 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"}> worldsheets
            ”) and instead of summing correlators of a 1d QFT, summing correlators of a 2-dimensional QFT, specifically a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
          %p
            The premise of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable world
            is that fundamental scattering processes such as observed in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+accelerator"} particle accelerator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            and which are to good approximation described by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            are more accurately described by such a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
          %p More conceptually, the premise is that
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
                is just an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
                (this is generally expected, independently of string theory)
            %li
              %p
                that a string perturbation series can provide its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
                – the
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
                are finite at each loop, hence are already
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalizations
                of the underlying
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
                amplitudes (the higher massive string oscillations serve as natural
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"} counterterms
                to the massless interactions of the effective low energy theory).
          %p
            While the string perturbation series is a well-defined expression analogous to the Feynman perturbation series, by itself, it lacks a
            %em conceptual
            property of the latter: the Feynman perturbation series is known, in principle, to be the approximation to
            = succeed "," do
              %em something
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+string+theory"} non-perturbative string theory
          %p
            This situation is analogous to the following simple setup: the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            can be approximated by the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/species"} species
          %p
            There are a host of educated guesses of what non-perturbative string theory might be if anything, but it remains unknown. At some point, the term
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            had been established for whatever that non-perturbative theory is, but even though it already has a name, it still remains unknown. (Or rather: its full incarnation remains unknown. What is well defined is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            with some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-brane"} M-brane
            effects and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+enhancement"} gauge enhancement
            included, and that is what is presently being studied under the name “M-theory”, see for instance at
            = succeed ";" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
          %p Therefore if the qualification “perturbative”/“non-perturbative” is suppressed, then the term “string theory” is quite ambiguous and has frequently led to misunderstanding. Perturbative string theory is a well-defined and formally suggestive variant of established perturbation theory in QFT. Non-perturbative string theory on the other hand is a hypothetical refinement of this perturbative theory of which there are maybe some hints, but which by and large remains mysterious, if it exists at all.
          %h4#WhyNopBranePerturbationTheory
            Then why not consider perturbative
            = succeed "-brane" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#WhatIsStringTheory"} above
            motivation of perturbative string theory as the evident result of replacing the definition of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            encoding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
            of particles, by 2-dimensional diagrams (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"}> Riemann surfaces
            ) encoding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            raises the evident question: why stop at strings? Why not consider an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            built as the sum of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            field theory for each
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+1
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} membrane
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 2
            = succeed "?" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-brane"} p-brane
          %p
            To answer this, it is again crucial to distinguish between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative theory
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            for many different values of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-brane" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 1
          %p The reason is that it is hard and seems impossible to make sense of this. There are two technical problems:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 1
                then the standard worldvolume action functionals (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nambu-Goto+action"}> Nambu-Goto action
                ), are not renormalizable;
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 1
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
                of p+1-manifolds are not controllable.
          %p
            So for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 1
            one a) does not know how to define the “Feynman amplitudes” and b) even if one did, one does not know how against what to integrate them.
          %p
            Each of these two problems in itself makes a
            = succeed "-brane" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 1
            be hard to come by.
          %p
            That is, incidentally, the very reason for the term “M-theory”. First, there had been the observation that the super 1-brane in 10d target spacetimes is accompanied by a 2-brane in 11d target spacetime, now called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            (for the history see
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Duff"} Mike Duff
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+World+in+Eleven+Dimensions"} The World in Eleven Dimensions
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membranes"} membranes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
            proposed to say “M-theory” as an abbreviation, not to commit himself to what exactly might be really going on, and leaving open for the future if “M” is for “membrane” or for something else:
          %blockquote
            %p
              As it has been proposed that the eleven-dimensional theory is a supermembrane theory but there are some reasons to doubt that interpretation, we will non-committally call it the M-theory, leaving to the future the relation of M to membranes. (
              %a{:href => "M-theory#HoravaWitten95"}> Hořava-Witten 95
              )
            %p
              %em M
              stands for
              = succeed "," do
                %em magic
              = succeed "," do
                %em mystery
              = succeed "," do
                %em membrane
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "M-theory#Witten95"} Witten 95
          %h3#WhatAreTheEquations What are the equations of string theory?
          %p
            All
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theories"} local field theories
            in physics are prominently embodied by key
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations"} equations
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"}> general relativity
            ) is essentially the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            is governed by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
          %p
            But
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            is not a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix+theory"} S-matrix theory
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsStringTheory"} What is string theory?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation"} equation
            that picks out the physical trajectories, it is given by a formula for how to compute
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitude"} probability amplitude
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi in
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{in}
            asymptotic states of strings coming in (into a particle collider experiment, say), scattering, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi out
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{out}
            other asymptotic string states emerging (and hitting a detector, say) is a sum over all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surfaces"} Riemann surfaces
            with
            = succeed "-punctures" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mi in
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mi out
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n_{in}, n_{out})
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            of the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            that defines the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+vacuum"} string vacuum
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsAStringVacuum"} What is a string vacuum?
          %p
            More in detail, a string background is equivalently a choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            of central charge 15 (a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"}> 2-spectral triple
            ”), and in terms of this the formula for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            element/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"} scattering amplitude
            for a bunch of asymptotic string states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi in
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi in
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi in
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi^1_{in}, \cdots, \psi^{n_{in}}_{in}
            coming in, and a bunch of states
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi out
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi out
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi out
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi^1_{out}, \cdots, \psi^{n_{out}}_{out}
            coming out is schematically of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi in
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi in
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi in
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi out
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi out
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi out
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %msup
                    %mi λ
                    %mi g
                  %munder
                    %mo ∫
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mi moduli
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi space
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mi of
                      %mrow
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi n
                                %mi in
                              %mo ,
                              %msub
                                %mi n
                                %mi out
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mi punctured
                          %mrow
                            %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mrow
                                  %mi super
                                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                  %mi Riemann
                                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                  %mi surfaces
                              %mrow
                                %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %mrow
                                      %msubsup
                                        %mi Σ
                                        %mi g
                                        %mrow
                                          %msub
                                            %mi n
                                            %mi in
                                          %mo ,
                                          %msub
                                            %mi n
                                            %mi out
                                  %mrow
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi of
                                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                      %mi genus
                                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                      %mi g
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi SCFT
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Correlator
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi over
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi Σ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi of
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi states
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi in
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi in
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi in
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi out
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ψ
                        %mi out
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi n
                            %mi out
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{\psi^1_{in}, \cdots, \psi^{n_{in}}_{in}, \psi^1_{out}, \cdots, \psi^{n_{out}}_{out}}
                  \\;=\;
                  \\underset{g \in \mathbb{N}}{\sum}
                  \\lambda^g
                  \\underset{
                  {moduli \; space \; of}
                  \\atop
                  {{(n_{in},n_{out}) punctured}
                  \\atop
                  {{super\; Riemann \; surfaces}
                  \\atop
                  {{\Sigma^{n_{in}, n_{out}}_g}
                  \\atop
                  {of\; genus\; g}}}}
                  }{
                  \\int
                  }
                  \\left(
                  SCFT \; Correlator \; over \; \Sigma \; of \; states\;
                  {\psi^1_{in}, \cdots, \psi^{n_{in}}_{in}, \psi^1_{out}, \cdots, \psi^{n_{out}}_{out}}
                  \\right)
          %p
            expressing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            element (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitude"}> scattering amplitude
            ) shown on the left as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+coupling+constant"} string coupling constant
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            the integrals over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Riemann+surfaces"} super Riemann surfaces
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-point functions) for the given incoming and outgoing string states.
          %p
            With more technical details filled in, this formula reads as follows: for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
            %a{:href => "#Polchinski01"} Polchinski 01, volume 1, equation (5.3.9)
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/StringSMatrixFromPolchinski.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            and for the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            %a{:href => "#Polchinski01"} Polchinski 01, volume 2, equation (12.5.24)
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/StringSMatrixSuperFromPolchinski.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            This is the equation that defines
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
          %p
            And this is of just the same form (cf. at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %em more complicated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            with labeling for all intermediate particles (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"}> virtual particles
            ) and with some choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            to make the integrals well defined, whereas here we simply sum over all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Riemann+surfaces"} super Riemann surfaces
            and that’s it. The different intermediate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"} virtual particles
            as well as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counterterms"} counterterms
            are all taken care of by the higher string modes, encoded in the worldsheet CFT correlators.
          %p
            There was a time in the 1960s, when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theorists
            around
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geoffrey+Chew"} Geoffrey Chew
            proposed that precisely such formulas for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            elements should be exactly what defines a quantum field theory, this and nothing else. The idea was to do away with an explicit concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            and local interactions, and instead declare that all there is to be said about physics is what is seen by particles that probe the physics by scattering through it. This is an intrinsically quantum approach, where there need not be any classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            defined in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            geometry. Instead, all there is a formula for the outcome of scattering experiments.
          %p
            Historically, this radical perspective fell out of fashion for a while with the success of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
            model in its formulation as as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            coming from an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            But fashions come and go, and the original idea of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geoffrey+Chew"} Geoffrey Chew
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            approach continues to make sense in itself, and it is this form of a physical theory that perturbative string theory is an example of.
          %p
            More recently, the S-matrix perspective becomes fashionable also in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tr%28%CF%95%C2%B3%29"} Tr(ϕ³)
            enjoy good structures in its scattering amplitudes which are essentially invisible in the vast summation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            that extract the S-matrix from the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/amplituhedron"} amplituhedron
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associahedron"} associahedron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that encode, for instance, the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wheeler+superspace"} Wheeler
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction"} wavefunction
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} universe
            (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmohedron"} cosmohedron
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraviolet+divergence"} UV
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infrared+divergence"} IR
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergent+series"} divergence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+integrals"} Feynman integrals
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+polytope"} Feynman polytope
          %p
            On the other hand, there
            %em is
            also an analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantized
            field-theory-with-equations for string scattering: this is called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo +
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo +
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 6
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo +
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Q \psi + \tfrac{1}{2} \psi \star \psi + \tfrac{1}{6} \psi \star \psi \star \psi + \cdots = 0
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            is the string field,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST operator
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
            is the string field star product.
          %p
            (For the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
            this is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "string+field+theory#Zwiebach"} Zwiebach 92, equation (4.46)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            this is in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "string+field+theory#Sen15"} Sen 15 equation (2.22)
          %p
            The string field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            has infinitely many components, one for each excitation mode of the string. Its lowest excitations are the modes that correspond to massless
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            around a background solution and together with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+curvature+corrections"} higher curvature corrections
          %h3#why_is_string_theory_controversial Why is string theory controversial?
          %p
            As a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            that is supposed to checked by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            and which predicts that all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            are secretly, if one probes at high enough
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstrings"} superstrings
          %p
            Current particle accelerator technology (notably the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            %em directly
            (at least for many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} models
            in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
          %p
            However,
            %em in principle
            and
            %em possibly
            there are indirect effects of string theory that would be probe-able with current
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            and if so, then the properties of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            (traditionally but not necessarily taken to be a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            that we do see in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            is pretty baroque in its field content, the hope here would be that a choice of fiber space could
            %em explain
            for instance that there are three generations of particles, if not even explain their couplings and masses.
          %p
            For decades there has been the more or less implicit idea that the number of choices of the fiber spaces is small. That would mean – or would have meant – that string theory can make predictions not just as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            does, namely about particle scattering
            %em given
            the nature of the particles, but even about that nature itself, hence that it could, in the extreme case, for instance
            %em predict the mass
            of the elementary particles.
          %p Notice that if so, this would be quite remarkable. For all we know, the interaction terms and masses of the fundamental particles could be just as coincidental as the number of planets in our solar system, and their distance from the sun (both of which were once speculated to follow from first principles of mathematics, which of course they do not).
          %p While there was never any real indication that such a prediction would be possible, later the idea became widespread that it seems indeed rather unlikely. If so, then we are back to the first point, namely 15 orders of magnitude and hence impractically far away from testing string theory directly.
          %p Then there are two possible standpoints, and they account for the controversy:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                If you want to learn right now about the fine detail of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
                and just that, for instance if you are a genuine particle physicist, then string theory may give you exactly no useful information whatsoever and all time spent on it is plain wasted. The frustration over the fact that there has been the claim that string theory may directly help with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUT
                building and other standard model physics and that none of this turns to work out is responsible for much of the criticism. And, by all accounts, rightly so.
            %li
              %p
                If however you feel more generally theoretically inclined, say because you are a theoretical physicist wondering about the possibilities of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                in general, or if you are even a mathematician, happy to handle theories that are not supposed have direct relation to
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %p String theory might be right as an assumption about the fundamental nature of the observable universe, and might at the same time still be entirely useless for experimentally based particle physics in the present age. In that case, if you are interested in experimental particle physics you should not expect much help in your lifetime. But if you are a theoretical physicist or a mathematician interested in broader conceptual questions, then it may be unwise to ignore the theory.
          %h3#HowDoesStringTheoryMakePredictions Does string theory make predictions? How?
          %p
            String theory makes predictions much as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            does, too: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            predicts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            once a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            in the theory has been chosen.
          %p
            Most every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            has parameters and makes predictions only after sufficiently many parameters have been fixed (i.e., specified) by measuring them in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %p
            For instance
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newton"} Newton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            says that the gravitational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            of a pointlike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            is proportional to the inverse square of the distance from that mass. This is the theory, the proportionality factor (now called
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+constant"} Newton's constant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %p
            In string theory as in any other theory of physics, it is the same general principle, only that the theory is much richer. There are lots of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            in string theory that make very detailed statements about the resulting physics. Moreover, many of these have good general agreement with presently observed data. One speaks of “semi-realistic models”, see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/string%20phenomenology#SemiRealisticModels"} Semi-realistic models in string theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model physics
          %p
            The remaining problem is the following, and this is not specific to string theory but faced by any theory that provides a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            of the standard model plus gravity (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"}> quantum gravity
            ): the problem is that after parameters have been fixed this way by finding a model that reproduces the standard model reasonably accurately, all the remaining properties of the model, hence the predictions of the model, tend to be at high energies (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"}> Planck scale
            ”) and hence not within reach of present
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            such as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
          %p
            This is a very general aspect of present particle physics: while
            %em theoretically
            it is fairly clear that the standard model plus gravity must have a UV-completion by
            = succeed "," do
              %em something
          %p
            But one technical speciality of string theory over QFT model building exists in either case: what in the standard model are external parameters put into a QFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli"} moduli
          %p
            The simple familiar example to compare this to is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            in Einstein gravity: one can either consider it as an external parameter, a constant real number coefficient in front of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            summand of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Hilbert+action"} Einstein-Hilbert Lagrangian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            with some potential and consider those solutions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            where this field is almost constant to good approximation. In such a case the field itself serves as an
            %em effective
            cosmological constant. (This is the mechanism behind the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
          %p
            In string theory this happens with
            %em all
            the parameters. (Except for one single constant: the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+coupling+constant"} string coupling constant
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "string+theory#Scales"} string theory – scales
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
          %p
            That is why in string theory instead of adjusting parameters one searches solutions. Since these are also called “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+vacua"}> string vacua
            ” (see at
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsAStringVacuum"} What is a string vacuum?
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
          %p
            In summary:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            built in string theory make predictions just as any other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model
            in theoretical physics does. The situation is actually better in that in principle the choice of model in string theory is constrained by the theory. While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            is just written to paper, when one reproduces approximations to it in string theory one has to check that various consistency conditions are satisfied which guarantee that the parameters assumed indeed do arise as configurations of the fields in the theory.
          %p
            Notice that there is no a priori reason in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that of all of the huge space of possible field theories (all
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangians"} local Lagrangians
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            – an “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"}> Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
            ”. This is not a prediction of QFT but a theoretical assumption based on experimental observation, and only after this assumption is made, and only after a few dozen further parameters are fixed by hand, does the standard model of particle physics start to make any predictions at all. On the other hand, string theory does predict this general form of action functionals: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theories"} effective field theories
            obtained in string theory are generally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theories"} gauge theories
            coupled to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %p Nevertheless, in spite of this higher predictivity of string theory in principle, in practice it has not yet led to much insight that would actually affect particle physics models in practice.
          %h4#AsideHowDoPhysicalTheorieyGenerallyMakePredictionsAnyway Aside: How do physical theories generally make predictions, anyway?
          %p
            It may be good to compare to established and (essentially) uncontroversial
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
          %p
            A good example to think of, in particular in comparison to string theory, is the theory of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"} Einstein
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
          %p
            Today there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            which is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            built in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "cosmic+inflation#ReferencesExperimentalEvidence"} cosmic inflation - Experimental evidence
          %p
            This model cannot be predicted by the theory of Einstein-gravity. It is one of many possible solutions, one point in a vastly infinitely dimensional space of solutions of general relativity. It is in turn based on the class of models known as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+models"} FRW models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
            and pressure of homogenous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            filling the universe, and the value of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            %em remaining
            parameters, such as here the fluctuations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+microwave+background+radiation"} cosmic microwave background radiation
            in a universe described by this model.
          %p
            This is the general pattern of
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong how predictions are made in physical theories
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                posit a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            %li
              %p
                specify some of the parameters of the theory – “build a
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            %li
              %p
                check what the theory demands for the remaining parameters – these are the
                %em predictions
                of that model in that theory;
            %li
              %p if experiment disagrees with the predictions then
              %ol
                %li
                  %p see if this can be repaired by modifying the model a little, hence by varying some of the originally fixed parameters;
                %li
                  %p if this is impossible or gets to be too complicated to the degree of feeling “unnatural”, then either look for an entirely different model or, if all fails, modify or eventually abandon the theory and find a new one. Then repeat.
          %p
            That this model building process is a matter of trial and error is well witnessed for instance by the early history of cosmology. Right after Einstein had found general relativity and the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %p Clearly the theory does not help us predict this, otherwise there would be no reason for this repeated process of trial and error. On the other hand, once we do fix these global parameters, the theory does predict plenty of further observations in the model, which can and have been checked.
          %p
            But curiously, the modern version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            with its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            (“dark energy”) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            component, asserts that the vast majority of all constituents of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            are in fact unobservable, except for their gravitational effect. This means: the parameters of the model can be made to fit observation, but only with the rather noteworthy consequence that the model now models something which to a large extent is not what it set out to model.
          %p
            At this point there are two options: either one can take it that the standard model of cosmology
            %em predicts
            the existence of vast amounts of dark matter and dark energy, because only with this assumption is the model consistent with observations (but with this assumption it is very well consistent with observation). On the other hand, one might feel that with all this dark matter assumed, the model has become too contrived to be “natural”, and that instead this is a hint that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of Einstein gravity is not quite right. This is sociologically currently a minority position, but it is certainly intellectually a possible standpoint; it goes by the name
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MOND"} MOND
          %p
            Similar descriptions can be given of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            that demands that the theory of fundamental particle physics has to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            coupled to Einstein-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"}> gravity
            , an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            of that theory is what is observed (though there are constraints on the possible gauge groups, from
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly+cancellation"} quantum anomaly cancellation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            appears in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            representations that it does (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"}> electrons
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrinos"} neutrinos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            in the model such as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yukawa+couplings"} Yukawa couplings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutrinos"} neutrinos
            and then the confirmation that there is indeed a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+boson"} Higgs boson
            sector. None of this could be derived from first principles of quantum field theory. Even for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+mechanism"} Higgs mechanism
            there are plenty of potential theoretical alternatives (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/technicolor"} technicolor
          %p
            This is why one speaks of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            and of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            and not of the “standard theory”. Because the theory is fixed in both cases,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein"} Einstein
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            it is just like this. One can write down
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} models
            that look like the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            coupled to gravity at low energy, and then see what the theory predicts as further observations. The difference here is that there are many more constraints on models in string theory (string theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
          %p
            However, what has often been considered attractive in string theory is that despite this,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            does reasonably constrain the vast possibility space of models in quantum field theory. While there is no reason in QFT that our world is described by Yang-Mills gauge theory and Einstein-gravity, this is precisely what models in string theory generically predict. This may not seem like a practically useful prediction given that we already
            %em assumed
            this since the 1915s and 1960s, respectively, by building it into our “standard models”, but from the standpoint of conceptual understanding over pure empirical measurement it might be regarded as a suggestive aspect that in string theory the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
            can be derived from a more fundamental theory, in the vast space of other possible
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangians"} local Lagrangians
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+action+principle"} spectral action principle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
            %em and
            provides a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            for it. The trouble is just that, by the nature of UV completions, these concern the behaviour at higher energy scales, and in this case at higher energy scales than can conceivably be measured in the near future. This is a general problem of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            and as such not specific to string theory.
          %p
            However, string theory has potential effects on at least the conceptual understanding of quantum field theory beyond direct measurement of high energy effects. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
            for more on this.
          %h3#IsStringTheoryTestable Is string theory testable?
          %p
            This question overlaps with the question
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#HowDoesStringTheoryMakePredictions"} How does string theory make predictions?
          %p
            In both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            as well as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            one “builds
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
            data in 2012, when the experiment ruled out more and more of the possible parameter space for one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+supersymmetry"} global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            somewhere at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electroweak+symmetry+breaking"} electroweak symmetry breaking
            scale (see also
            %em
              %a{:href => "#DoesSTPredictSupersymmetry"} Does string theory predict supersymmetry
            below). So all this was testable, has been tested and turned out to be wrong.
          %p
            So
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model
            building in string theory is much as in QFT. When a model is ruled out, it does not necessarily mean that string theory is ruled out or that QFT is ruled out, but it means that the possibilities for adjusting the free parameters in the theory are being reduced.
          %p
            To see that this is a common scientific process, it may help to look at some important historical examples. For instance shortly after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein"} Einstein
            proposed the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            now named after him, he proposed a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model
            within that theory. Since he thought back then that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            was static, he chose a free parameter of his theory, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+model"} FRW model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
          %p This kind of model-building process happens within string theory, too: by iteration the models are being tested, discarded or adjusted, tested again, etc.
          %p
            Actually, string theory model building is more constrained than plain QFT model building, due to the fact that at the heart of it there are
            %em no
            free parameters, since all parameters are instead
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            of the theory, as mentioned before in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#HowDoesStringTheoryMakePredictions"} How does string theory make predictions?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            that can be further adjusted as the energy scale is increased, but is already a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            really are solutions to the theory. They have all been checked to be so only in some approximation.)
          %p
            An example of how string theory is more constrained in its model building than effective QFT keeps the community busy since 1998: then it was observed experimentally that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            these days is small but positive. But accommodating a positive cosmological constant into string theoretic models is harder than negative cosmological constant. So this observation tested a large region of string model building and found it to be wrong. But as for the example of Einstein’s “biggest blunder” above, even with all these models ruled out, the theory is still not ruled out (maybe one day with more observations it will!) Instead, as in the historical example, the failure of the favorite models lead to new theoretical activity in understanding the theory and its remaining possible models. All this talk about “metastable vacua”, etc since in string theory originates in this experimental observation in 1998.
          %p
            On the other hand, no other theoretical framework was equally tested by this astronomical observation. In all other existing theoretical frameworks you simply take a pen and change the sign of the cosmological constant by hand. The theory does not control it, it’s a free parameter. So it seems justifiable to say that string theory is actually
            %em more testable
            than other existing theoretical frameworks. Of course to appreciate what this means one has to pay attention to what it means to test a theory by testing all its parameter space of models, as illustrated by the Einstein “biggest blunder”-example.
          %h3#IsStringTheoryCausal Is string theory causal, given that it is not local on the string scale?
          %p
            In an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            theory such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            (see above at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsStringTheory"} What is string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causality"} causality
            is embodied by the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            shows certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+function"} analyticity features
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            approach to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is often referred to as “the analytic S-matrix”).
          %p
            Since, as opposed to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causality"} causality
            might be violated in string theory. However, computation shows that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitude"} string scattering
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            comes out suitably analytic and causal (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "causal+locality#Martinec95"} Martinec 95
          %p
            A detailed analysis for how this comes about has been given in (
            %a{:href => "causal+locality#ErlerGross04"}> Erler-Gross 04
            ). They write:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Perhaps then it comes as a surprise that critical string theory produces an analytic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
              consistent with macroscopic causality. In absence of any other known theoretical mechanism which might explain this, despite appearances one is lead to believe that string interactions must be, in some sense, local.
          %p and
          %blockquote
            %p We find that string theory avoids problems with nonlocality in a surprising way. In particular, we find that the Witten vertex is “local enough” to allow for a nonsingular description of the theory which is completely local along a single null direction.
          %p and
          %blockquote
            %p unlike lightcone string field theory, it is clear that cubic string field theory at least has a local limit where all spacetime coordinates are taken to the midpoint. We investigate this limit with a careful choice of regulator and show that at any stage the theory is nonsingular but arbitrarily close to being local and manifestly causal. We believe that the existence of this limit, though singular, must account for the macroscopic causality of the string S-matrix. Thus, string theory is local enough to avoid the inconsistencies of a theory which is acausal and nonlocal in time, but is nonlocal enough to make string theory different from quantum field theory
          %h3#DoesSTPredictSupersymmetry Does string theory predict supersymmetry?
          %p
            To understand this question and its answer, it is important to know that in general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetries"} symmetries
            in physics (and in mathematics) come in a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+symmetry"} local
            and in a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+symmetry"} global
            flavor. For instance the
            %em theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is a theory which has as a
            %em local symmetry
            the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+group"} Poincaré group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            of the theory – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            – may or may not have global Lorentz symmetry (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+vector+fields"}> Killing vector fields
            ”). In fact, the generic solution to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            has
            %em no
            global Lorentz symmetry left. Indeed, global Lorentz symmetry of spacetime with all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matter"} matter
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            fields in it would mean that the world looks the same if we arbitrarily translate in some direction, or arbitrarily rotate in some plane. It would be rather bizarre to live in a spacetime with such a property!
          %p
            (Mathematically the distinction is this: given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+space"} coset space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+geometry"} Klein geometry
            has global
            = succeed "-symmetry," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometries"} Cartan geometries
            have local
            = succeed "-symmetry.)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Now
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            refers to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+group"} super Lie group
            extension of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            has
            %em local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            just as Einstein gravity has local Lorentz symmetry, but the general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            in supergravity has no
            %em global
            supersymmetry left. This is just as unlikely as, and directly analous to, a spacetime having a global translation symmetry.
          %p
            (Mathematically this is now the situation of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Cartan+geometry"} super Cartan geometry
          %p
            Contrary to that,
            %em local
            supersymmetry is rather generic in low dimensions. For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            theory of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particle"} spinning particle
            – such as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            – is locally supersymmetric. (See the references
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/spinning%20particle#WorldlineSupersymmtryReferences"} here
            %em local
            supersymmetry has been experimentally verified for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            were first observed to exist, which is since the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stern-Gerlach+experiment"} Stern-Gerlach experiment
          %p
            Similarly, if one proceeds from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particles"} spinning particles
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+strings"} spinning strings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            automatically satifies local
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %em found
            historically: people wrote down the obvious action functional for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+strings"} spinning strings
            which was just intended to produce
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            and then it was noticed that this exhibits a curious extra “super”-symmetry. Ever since the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+string"} spinning string
            is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
          %p
            The miracle that then happens is that while the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particles"} spinning particles
            (which is fermionic
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+strings"} spinning strings
            (which is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            with fermions)
            %em does
            itself also exhibit local supersymmetry: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            induced by spinning strings is not just
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %p
            (This result is known as the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goddard-Thorn+no-ghost+theorem"} Goddard-Thorn no-ghost theorem
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GSO+projection"} GSO projection
          %p
            So: string theory implies that if there are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            at all, then there is
            %em local
            supersymmetry, hence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi strings
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi &amp;
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi fermions
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi supergravity
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  strings \;\; \&amp; \;\; fermions \;\;\; \Rightarrow \;\;\; supergravity
          %p
            On the other hand, any
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            in string theory may or may not retain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+supersymmetry"} global supersymmetry
            at some energy scale, after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spontaneous+symmetry+breaking"} spontaneous symmetry breaking
          %p
            In fact the generic model has a priori no reason to preserve any low energy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+supersymmetry"} global supersymmetry
            below the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck+scale"} Planck scale
            at all (see e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "split+supersymmetry#GiudiceStrumia11"} Giudice-Strumia 11, p. 1-2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            does not preserve any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            symmetry. The condition that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            of 10-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            to 4d exhibits precisely one global supersymmetry is equivalent to the compactification space being a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} supersymmetry and Calabi-Yau manifolds
            %em require
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} Calabi-Yau manifolds
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenological
            reasons it is (or was) expected that our observed world exhibits global low-energy supersymmetry.
          %p
            However, recently arguments for a theoretical preference for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N=1
            supersymmetric compactifications have been advanced after all (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Twisted+Cohomotopy+implies+M-theory+anomaly+cancellation"}> FSS19, Sec. 3.4
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Bobby+Acharya#Acharya19"} Acharya 19
          %p
            The role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+supersymmetry"} local supersymmetry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"}> supergravity
            ) which superstring theory does predict is quite different from that of low energy global supersymmetry. Notice that as soon as there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            in the world it follows that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is described by
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            defining any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum field theory
            containing them. Now
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            alone does not mean that there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            on anything, which is what is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
          %p
            On the other hand, in low
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            dimension this tends to be inevitable. For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            theory of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+particle"} spinning particle
            (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            %em
              %a{:href => "spinning+particle#WorldlineSupersymmetry"} spinning particle - Worldline supersymmetry
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/spinning+particle#WorldlineSupersymmtryReferences"} spinning particle - Worldline supersymmetry - References
          %p
            Similarly, the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinning+string"} spinning string
            was not originally intended to be supersymmetric, but just so happens to come out this way. The special phenomenon as opposed to the superparticle is that the worldsheet supersymmetry of the string
            %em implies
            that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            is also locally supersymmetric, hence is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %h3#WhatIsAStringVacuum What is a string vacuum?
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            is the information needed to turn a product of field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            such as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x) \mathbf{\Phi}^b(y)
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            (or rather:
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+function"} generalized function
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+function"} n-point function
            (here
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-point+function"} 2-point function
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hadamard+propagator"} Hadamard propagator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Φ
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle \mathbf{\Phi}^a(x) \mathbf{\Phi}^b(y)\rangle
          %p
            which may be regarded as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitude"} probability amplitude
            for a quantum in state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            at spacetime point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            to turn into a quantum in state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            at spacetime point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %em in the given state
            that the fields are in, which is defined thereby (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state+in+AQFT"} state in AQFT
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            of field theories these
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            arise from a 1-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"}> worldline
            ” of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particle"}> virtual
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            running from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            Now by the very definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            whose dynamics is now encoded in a 2d field theory on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            of strings, specifically a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+superconformal+field+theory"} 2d superconformal field theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"}> 2d SCFT
            ) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+charge"} central charge
            15. Hence now it is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
            which defines the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            that the perturbative string theory is in. This is then called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacuum"} perturbative string theory vacuum
          %p
            In practice full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            are hard to construct, and often one considers them by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theories"} perturbation theory
            of a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"}> sigma-model
            ” which is defined by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            manifold equipped with extra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            (e.g. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            etc.). It turns out that to low order these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            configurations that define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            are given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solutions"} solutions
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theories (e.g.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
          %p
            Therefore often such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            solutions equipped with some extra data that makes them consistent CFT backgrounds at higher order are referred to as
            %em vacua
            for string theory. But this is in general a coarse approximation. The full vacua are the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            that define the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            theory of the string.
          %p
            The collection of all string vacua, possibly subject to some assumptions, has come to be called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            %em
              %a{:href => "#WhatDoesItMeanToSayStringTheoryHasALandscapeOfSolutions"} What does it mean to say that string theory has a landscape of vacua?
          %h3#BackgroundDependence How/why does string theory depend on “backgrounds”?
          %p
            To answer this question one needs – as discussed at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#WhatIsStringTheory"} What is string theory?
            – to distinguish between
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+string+theory"} non-perturbative string theory
          %p
            Perturbative string theory, being a variant of traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em about a background
          %p
            To stick with the (close) analogy to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            mentioned before in
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsStringTheory"} What is string theory?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            involves a choice of point on the real line around which the Taylor series is developed. The series itself represents the original function restricted to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal
            neighbourhood of that point.
          %p
            This example also serves to illustrate in which sense a perturbation series “depends” on a choice of background: for a given smooth function and for two points on the real line that lie within the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence+radius"} convergence radius
            of the Taylor series of that function around the respective other point, the expansion does
            %em not
            depend on the “choice of background” in the sense that the value of one series evaluated at a given point equals the value of the other series evaluated at the same point. The only restriction to this statement is that some points may lie outside of the convergence radius.
          %p
            For QFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            we have essentially the same situation: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            of the theory are expressed as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            and in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal
            approximations to the true non-perturbative correlators expanded about zero coupling and vanishing Planck’s constant. At that 0-point the theory is non-interacting and “classical”, meaning that this is the point of a solution to the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of the non-interacting theory. Such a solution is also called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            or a
            %em background
            of the quantum theory. Notably in theories containing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (such as string theory), such a background involves a solutions to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            on which all further perturbative quantum fields propagate.
          %p
            By the above logic, while the specific perturbation series depends on the choice of this classical solution, hence the choice of “background” or of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            of that formal series is necessarily 0, see the discussion at
            %em
              %a{:href => "#NonConvergenceOfPerturbationSeries"} Isn’t it fatal that the string perturbation series does not converge?
            )
          %p
            By design, all this applies also to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
          %p
            As mentioned before, there is the idea that perturbative string theory is indeed the perturbative approximation to an as-yet unknown
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+string+theory"} non-perturbative string theory
          %p
            Consistency arguments for this speculation have been given in (
            %a{:href => "#Witten"}> Witten xy
            ). A theoretical framework for formalizing these questions is
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
          %p
            For more on what perturbative string theory around a given such background looks like, see also the question
            %em
              %a{:href => "#RelationToGravity"} How is string theory related to the theory of gravity?
          %h3#InsightInParticlePhysics Did string theory provide any insight relevant in experimental particle physics?
          %p Yes. One curious aspect of string theory is that independently of its role as a source for models in particle physics, it provides connections in the space of all possible quantum field theories: lots of different quantum field theories (many of them highly unrealistic as phenomenology goes, but interesting for theoretical investigations) appear as different limits and special cases inside string theory, and their embedding into a single framework this way explains many unexpected relations between them. One of this has led to recent progress simply in computational tools of perturbation series. LHC physicists claimed that without the insight from string theory, the evaluation software used at the LHC could not have been precise enough to see the Higgs in the data.
          %p Details on this are linked to at
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theory results applied elsewhere
              \.
          %p
            This is maybe the example most directly related to experimental physics, but there are various other relations (“dualities”) between QFTs learned from or better understood with string theory. (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seiberg+duality"} Seiberg duality
            for instance, long list will go here…)
          %h3#InsightsIntoCosmology Does string theory tell us anything about cosmology, such as the Big bang or cosmic inflation?
          %p
            No. Looking back at the answer to
            %em
              %a{:href => "#WhatIsStringTheory"} What is string theory?
            we have that the only thing really defined is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            – and quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            is clearly insufficient for discussion of strongly coupled phenomena such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmological
            phenomena (as opposed to those tiny excitations studied in particle accelerators).
          %p
            More precisely: given that perturbative string theory does describe classical gravitational backgrounds with small quantum fluctuations about them, and given that this is already all that traditional cosmology considers, certainly all of traditional cosmology can sensibly be modeled in string theory. But perturbative string theory cannot say much about the long-expected strong quantum corrections to gravitational effects in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
          %p This hasn’t stopped people from making lots of speculations, and there is something like a “field” called “string cosmology” in that you find authors writing about this, giving talks and lectures about it. But this is a bit like a soccer match with a ping-pong ball.
          %p
            What would be needed here is an understanding of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+string+theory"} non-perturbative string theory
          %p
            On the other hand, in theories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            such as string theory, there are some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            that are independent of the coupling constant (called “protected”): the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPS+states"} BPS states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            is set to a finite value, then one can know the value of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative theory
            without even knowing that non-perturbative theory itself. This is the mechanism by which perturbative string theory does make statements about
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+hole+entropy"} black hole entropy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes"} black holes
            by their very nature are strongly coupled gravitational configurations. More on how this works is at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes+in+string+theory"} black holes in string theory
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} singularity
            involved in back holes is a similar kind of singularity as that involved in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/big+bang"} big bang
            one might think that some analogous method is useful in the latter case. But if so, it has not surfaced so far.
          %h3#RelationshipBetweenQuantumFieldTheoryAndStringTheory What is the relationship between string theory and quantum field theory?
          %p Recall from some of the previous answers:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} Perturbative string theory
                is defined to be the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
                which are obtained by summing
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
                of a 2d
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SCFT"} superconformal field theory
                of central charge -15 over all genera and moduli of (punctured)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Riemann+surfaces"} super Riemann surfaces
            %li
              %p
                Perturbative quantum field theory is defined to be the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
                which are obtained by applying the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
                to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
                – which equivalently, by
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
                of 1d field theories (of particles) over all loop orders of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+graphs"} Feynman graphs
          %p
            So the two are different. But for any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            one can ask if there is a non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalizable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"} local Lagrangian
            such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
            reproduce the given perturbation series at sufficiently low
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
            of the theory defined by the original perturbation series (which, if
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalized
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
          %p
            Now one can ask which effective quantum field theories arise this way as approximations to string perturbation series. It turns out that only rather special ones do. For instance those that arise all look like
            = succeed "-free" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac theory
            (consistent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            plus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            plus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+coupling"} minimally-coupled
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ising+model"} Ising model
          %p
            (Sometimes these days it is forgotten that QFT is much more general than the gauge theory plus gravity plus fermions that is seen in what is just the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            On the other hand now, within the restricted area of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac+theories"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFTs"} 2d CFTs
            one can obtain a large portion of the possible flavors of these theories in the low energy effective approximation. Lots of kinds of gauge groups, lots of kinds of particle content, lots of kinds of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+invariance"} modular invariance
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac+theories"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac theories
            as possible candidates for low energy effective quantum field theories approximating a string perturbation series. The space of them has been given a name (before really being understood, in good tradition…) and that name is, for better or worse, the “Swampland”.
          %h3#NonConvergenceOfPerturbationSeries Isn’t it fatal that the string perturbation series does not converge?
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            computed in string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            are thought to be term-wise (loop-wise, hence for each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+surface"} Riemann surface
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
            over all these contributions diverges. Does this mean that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            is unrealistic from the get go?
          %p
            No. On the contrary, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            of every QFT of interest is supposed to have vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            (
            %a{:href => "pQFT#Dyson52"}> Dyson 52
            ) and be just an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            and if the series had a finite radius of convergence, there would be also
            %em negative
            values of the coupling for which the correlators are convergent. See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative effect
            for more.
          %p
            So the non-convergence of the string perturbation series is just as it should be in QFT: a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            in quantum field theory is generally an
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
            that approximates the “actual” value (the actual
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+functions"} correlation functions
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+expansion"} asymptotic expansion
          %p
            The important property of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            is rather that they (plausibly) come out
            %em termwise
            finite (proven so for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string+theory"} bosonic string theory
            and the first orders of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring+theory"} superstring theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalized
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#HowDoStringsModelMassiveParticles"} How do strings model massive particles?
          %p
            So the convergence/divergence of the string perturbation theory is of the same kind as for instance in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            that appears in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a{:href => "perturbation+theory#DivergenceConvergence"} perturbation theory – divergence/convergence
            and especially see also the
            %a{:href => "perturbation+theory#ReferencesDivergenceConvergence"} references there
            and see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative effect
          %h3#HowDoStringsModelMassiveParticles How do strings model massive particles?
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            species corresponds to a different excitation mode of one single species of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+string"} fundamental string
            %em massless
            and receive a – comparatively low – mass only via a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+mechanism"} Higgs mechanism
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            therefore all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            correspond to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            excitations of strings. Indeed, the ground state excitations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            are massless (while that of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
            in fact has negative mass squared and hence models a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tachyon"} tachyon
            particle). This is a nontrivial statement due to the nature of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+harmonic+oscillators"} quantum harmonic oscillators
            and follows from a careful analysis of quantum effects.
          %p
            For instance, in simple situations the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            particles of spin 1, such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
          %p
            Every excited mode of a string however corresponds to a particle of comparatively huge mass, not meant to be close to any particle mass ever observed. So the infinite tower of higher string excitations is not meant to be directly observable in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particles"} virtual particles
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            is what renders these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
            loopwise finite: they are the counterterms that exhibit perturbative string theory as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalized
            perturbation theory. See also the discussion at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#NonConvergenceOfPerturbationSeries"} Isn’t it fatal that the string perturbation series does not converge?
          %h3#RelationToGravity How is string theory related to the theory of gravity?
          %p
            The technical statement is that the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            defined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
            (namely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+supergravity"} heterotic supergravity
            or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
          %p This means the following:
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            between given incoming and outgoing asymptotic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            of free
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
            abstractly defined by summing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlation+function"} correlation function
            of a 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SCFT"} SCFT
            over all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Riemann+surfaces"} super Riemann surfaces
            with given punctures.
          %p
            One tends to think of this sum heuristically as computing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+amplitudes"} probability amplitudes
            of all possible ways that the incoming strings can come in, interact with each other in a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+gauge+field"} background
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            such when we compute
            %em its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            by the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            rules of expansion about a solution to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            at low
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            which effectively approximates the string dynamics at low energy. (See also above
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#BackgroundDependence"} Why/how does string theory depend on “backgrounds”?
          %p
            In words this means: the “stringy” process defined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            looks in good approximation at low energy as such-and-such interactions of such-and-such
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
          %p
            And if one now computes what this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            defined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            is, then one finds that it generically is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            version of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            Notice that an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+quantum+field+theory"} effective quantum field theory
            (and this one is no exception) is not in general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalizable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            that provides these counterterms: the higher-energy interactions of the string provide the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            of its effective low energy theory of gravity+-Yang-Mills theory. One says that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+perturbation+series"} string perturbation series
            provides a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
            (hence for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
          %p
            By designs of what
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            are, all this are statements in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            and in perturbation theory. Notice that this is not some secret bug in string theory, but is so by the very definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
          %p
            The fact that
            %em perturbatively
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} UV-completion
            of perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            may of course make one want to find something like
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+string+theory"} non-perturbative string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+correspondence"} AdS-CFT correspondence
            provides a non-perturbative extension of the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            by
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory"} perturbative string theory
            which is a rather well-defined and conceptually well-understood.
          %h3#StabilityOfKKCompactification Do the extra dimensions lead to instability of 4 dimensional spacetime?
          %p
            The parameters of size and shape of the compactified dimensions in string theory, and in fact in any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+compactification"} Kaluza-Klein compactification
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli"} moduli
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} metric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            and hence are dynamical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            that evolve. The problem of their stability, hence the question whether there are dynamical mechanisms that make for instance the size of the compactified space remain stably at a given value, is famous as the problem of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stabilization"} moduli stabilization in string theory
          %p
            This problem used to be open until around 2002. Then it was realized that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+values"} vacuum expectation values
            (VEVs) of the higher form fields (“fluxes”) present in string theory generically induce effective potentials for moduli that may stabilize them, at least for fluctuations that preserve the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+holonomy"} special holonomy
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} CY-compactifications
            of string theory or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} G₂-holonomy compactifications
            for M-theory).
          %p
            For
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} type IIB string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            this was argued in the influential article
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "moduli+stabilization#KKLT03"} KKLT 03
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "moduli+stabilization#Acharya02"} Acharya 02
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of type II string theory vacua
            (see also
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#WhatDoesItMeanToSayStringTheoryHasALandscapeOfSolutions"} below
          %h3#WhatDoesItMeanToSayStringTheoryHasALandscapeOfSolutions What does it mean to say that string theory has a “landscape of solutions”?
          %p
            Being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            (see
            %em
              %a{:href => "string%20theory%20FAQ#WhatIsStringTheory"} What is string theory?
            above), there are perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            for each solution of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective
            background theory of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super-gravity"}> super
            )-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
            (see also
            %em
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/string%20theory%20FAQ#BackgroundDependence"} How/why does string theory depend on backgrounds?
            above).
          %p
            In fact, more precisely, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacuum"} perturbative string theory vacuum
            for string perturbation theory is a choice of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} super
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+charge"} central charge
            15 (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsAStringVacuum"} What is a string vacuum?
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-spectral+triple"} 2-spectral triple
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+equations"} Yang-Mills equations
            (for instance “modular invariance” of the 2d theory, etc.). As a result, it is considerably harder to find a backround
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+vacuum"} string vacuum
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            than for its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV-completion"} non UV-complete
            non-renormalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
          %p
            Due to these strong constraints, there had for many decades be a wide spread hope among some (many) string theorists that these constraints are in fact so strong as to maybe essentially uniquely single out one small class of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-mechanism"} KK-mechanism
            theory like string theory determine the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            content in the low dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            There had never been a formal argument for this hope, but this hope has influenced both the community and its public perception. Then in the 1990s (only) string theorists began to start solving some of these extra constraints that string theory imposes for consistent
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenological
            constraint that the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli"}> moduli
            ” of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK+compactification"} KK compactification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dilaton"} dilaton
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            and its many cousins, become
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} massive
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LHC"} LHC
          %p
            One way to deal with this is to consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} models
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            which have nontrivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            configurations in the internal space. It turns out that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strengths"} field strengths
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
            in the compact space induce a potential energy for some or all of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+fields"} moduli fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            for them in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
          %p
            By a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            condition, the periods of these “flux fields” have to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            and hence form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+space"} discrete space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theories"} field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
            for a flux field over some internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"} cycle
            is some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            number.
          %p
            Hence given some internal manifold, the number of choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} models
            with fluxes is the product of some finite number, taken over each of the (still finite) classes of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
          %p
            Traditionally one considered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK+compactification"} KK compactification
            on real 6-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} Calabi-Yau manifolds
            (see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+Calabi-Yau+manifolds"} supersymmetry and Calabi-Yau manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 10
                    %mn 500
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 10^{500}
            many ways to choose the flux fields in such a flux compactification of string theory.
          %p
            While this number is large, for appreciating this number it is important to notice that typically the spaces of solutions to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} physical theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
            %em infinite dimensional
            continuous spaces. In view of this even a large finite number of solutions still makes a very small space of solutions, compared to the generic spaces of solutions that one traditionally deals with in physics.
          %p
            Nevertheless, due to that latent and long-time hope that maybe the full constraints on string theory vacua are so strong such as to only leave a handful, this number (obtained with plenty of assumptions and hand-waving and estimating, as it were) gave rise to a wide-spread feeling that the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacua"} vacua
            of string theory is “vast”, and the word
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            became popular and accepted for this space – for what among sober mathematicians would just have been called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
          %p Ever since the public discussion shows a strong preference towards being worried about a “landscape problem” of string theory.
          %p
            One might argue that instead of worrying much about a hand-waving statement which even at face value is “better” than what one sees in generic field theories, energy might more fruitfully be invested into getting a better technical handle of what the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            – and hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            – actually is.
          %p For instance the authors of one of the few research programs these days to actually study the subtle nature of string theory backgrounds
          %ul
            %li#DistlerFreedMoore09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Distler"}> Jacques Distler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"} Daniel Freed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Moore"} Gregory Moore
              = succeed "," do
                %em Orientifold Précis
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              (eds.),
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/bookstore?fn=20&arg1=pspumseries&ikey=PSPUM-83"} volume 83
              AMS (2011) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0906.0795"}> arXiv:0906.0795
              )
          %p
            write on page 9 of their accurate study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifold"} orientifold
            backgrounds:
          %blockquote
            %p We hope that our formulation of orientifold theory can help clarify some aspects of and prove useful to investigations in orientifold compactifications, especially in the applications to model building and the “landscape.” In particular, our work suggests the existence of topological constraints on orientifold compactifications which have not been accounted for in the existing literature on the landscape.
          %p
            In summary then, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"} landscape of string theory vacua
            is essentially the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFTs"} 2d SCFTs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
            of the
            %em infinite-dimensional
            spaces of solutions of a typical theory of physics.
          %h3#IsStringTheoryRigorous Is string theory mathematically rigorous?
          %p
            The core of perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            has a mathematically rigorous formulation. In fact much of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
            and mathematical insight into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            as such has been gained from the study of the low-dimensional QFTs that constitute the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            and the various
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            in the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"}> FQFT
            ” flavor (roughly dual to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            picture) historically originates in insights gained in the study of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"}> topological
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            (namely the Moore-Seiberg axioms). On the other hand, the attempted implementations and applications of core string theory are vast and numerous, and when it finally comes to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            the usual level of rigor is just that common among practicing quantum field theorists. On the far end, deep aspects of string theory that are felt by many researchers to be of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysical
            relevance, such as the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/landscape+of+string+theory+vacua"}> landscape of string theory vacua
            ” (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#WhatDoesItMeanToSayStringTheoryHasALandscapeOfSolutions"} above
          %p More in detail:
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            may be obtained cleanly via the mathematical sound process of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "string#ReferencesSymplecticGeometryAndGeometricQuantization"} string – Symplectic geometry and Geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Weyl+anomaly"} Weyl anomaly
            of the string is formally understood in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} anomalous action functionals
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "conformal+anomaly#Freed86"} Freed 86 2.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstructions"} obstructions
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalies"}> quantum anomalies
            ) in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            such as notably the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freed-Witten-Kapustin+anomaly"} Freed-Witten-Kapustin anomaly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstructions"} obstructions
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory#ContributionDistlerFreedMoore"} Distler-Freed-Moore 09
          %p
            Particularly well analyzed are the two special sectors of first quantized string theory, that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+conformal+field+theory"} rational conformal field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            manifolds – the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wess-Zumino-Witten+model"} Wess-Zumino-Witten model
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+strings"} topological strings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFTs
            which have been subject to complete mathematical classification (in the same sense in which mathematicians for instance do the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classification+of+finite+simple+groups"} classification of finite simple groups
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS+formalism"} FRS formalism
          %p
            For the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
            much more is true. The topological string has effectively become a subject in pure mathematics, with its rigorous axiomatization via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            version of the
            = succeed "-theorem," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+duality"} geometric Langlands duality
            etc. Here it is maybe noteworthy that all this mathematical insight into string physics rests on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            (the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string"} topological string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category"} (infinity,n)-category
            theory). See also the question
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#HowDoesStringTheoryInvolveHomotopyTheory"} How does string theory involve homotopy theory, higher geometry and higher category theory?
          %p
            But the
            = succeed "-axiomatics" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a{:href => "vertex+operator+algebra#AsOperadAlgebras"} Huang’s theorem
            shows that the familiar description of physical string via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebra"} vertex operator algebra
            is an instance of the
            = succeed "-formalization." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRS+formalism"} FRS formalism
            these two formalizations,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebras"} vertex operator algebras
            (via their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+categories"} modular tensor categories
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            combined via the rigorous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} AdS3-CFT2 and CS-WZW correspondence
            give the classification of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+CFT"} rational CFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holography"} holography
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
            is rigorous, of course this is far, far from true in higher dimensions.) This means that the rigorous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the string in these approaches is “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"}> holographically
            ” encoded in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            of 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            is identified with the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"}> correlators
            ) of the string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/WZW-model"} WZW-model
            on this surface. Here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of Chern-Simons theory
            is one of the best analyzed quantizations of a non-trivial rich QFT.
          %p
            In summary this is a level of rigour with which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            of the string is understood which is well beyond of what one typically encounters for non-trivial interacting (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"}> non-free
            ) QFT. And this is full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            (on the
            = succeed "!)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
          %p
            From here on, also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+field+theory"} string field theory
            (its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"} L-infinity algebras
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+n-algebras"} Lie n-algebras
            for
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \to \infty
          %p
            Of course what does not have a rigorous formulation, not even a good non-rigorous formulation, is any conjectured non-perturbative (in
            = succeed "!)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#WhatIsStringTheory"} What is string theory
          %p
            A snapshot of the state of the art of rigorous foundations of string theory as of 2011 is in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
          %h3#what_does_it_mean_to_say_that_string_theory_depends_on_miracles_such_as_anomaly_cancellation_and_avoidance_of_divergences What does it mean to say that string theory depends on ‘miracles’, such as anomaly cancellation and avoidance of divergences?
          %p (…)
          %h3#HowDoesStringTheoryInvolveHomotopyTheory How does string theory involve homotopy theory, higher geometry and higher category theory?
          %p
            Most of the major frameworks of theoretical physics go along with a certain field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            that naturally formalizes them. For instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            today is understood as being described by the mathematics of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is described by
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            by what is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} Chern-Weil theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            involves all these physical and mathematical ingredients, but in a “higher dimensional” way. This “higher dimensional mathematics” is known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
          %p
            At the heart of it, this increase in “mathematical dimension” is simply a reflection of the fact that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            is one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            higher than a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            but of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is naturally captured by a higher dimensional mathematical structures known as an
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-category"} n-category
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            one has a 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+states"} spaces of states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-modules"} 2-modules
          %p
            But string theory is really about the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/second+quantization"} second quantization
            of these 2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            field theories, and it turns out that under this passage other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            theories of all kinds of dimensions appear, too: the various “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"}> branes
            ”. Accordingly,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-categories"} n-categories
            for various values of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Much of this relation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"}> homotopy theory
            , see at:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-categories"} n-categories
            did not really exist yet, just a vague feeling that such a theory ought to exist. What was prevalent in string theory then was a feeling that
            %em some
            kind of new mathematics would be necessary to capture the nature of the theory.
          %blockquote
            %p#Witten21stCenturyQuote
              Back in the early ’70s, the Italian physicist,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniele+Amati"} Daniele Amati
              reportedly said that string theory was part of 21st-century physics that fell by chance into the 20th century. I think it was a very wise remark. (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/elegant/view-witten.html"} Nova interview 2003
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.sns.ias.edu/~witten/papers/string.pdf"} American Scientist Astronomy Issue 2002
          %p For the moment, see the following for more:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Branislav+Jurco"}> Branislav Jurco
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"} Christian Saemann
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Wolf"} Martin Wolf
              %p
                %em> Higher Structures in M-Theory
                ,
              %p
                Introduction to
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Structures+in+M-Theory+2018"} Higher Structures in M-Theory 2018
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02807"} arXiv:1903.02807
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1002/prop.201910001"} doi:10.1002/prop.201910001
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Domenico+Fiorenza"}> Domenico Fiorenza
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/The+rational+higher+structure+of+M-theory"} The rational higher structure of M-theory
              %p
                in
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em
                    Proceedings of the
                    %a{:href => "http://www.maths.dur.ac.uk/lms/"} LMS-EPSRC Durham Symposium
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Structures+in+M-Theory+2018"} Higher Structures in M-Theory 2018
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.02834"} arXiv:1903.02834
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                ,
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Introduction+to+Higher+Supergeometry"} Introduction to Higher Supergeometry
              %p
                Lectures at
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Structures+in+M-Theory+2018"} Higher Structures in M-Theory 2018
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#References References
          %p
            Textbooks on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            include the following (for more see at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/books+about+string+theory"} books about string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Duff"} Mike Duff
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+World+in+Eleven+Dimensions"}> The World in Eleven Dimensions
                  \: Supergravity, Supermembranes and
                  = succeed "-theory" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi M
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                ,
              %p
                IoP 1999 (
                %a{:href => "https://www.crcpress.com/The-World-in-Eleven-Dimensions-Supergravity-supermembranes-and-M-theory/Duff/9780750306720"}> publisher
                )
            %li#Polchinski01
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joseph+Polchinski"}> Joseph Polchinski
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+theory"} String theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Etingof"} Pavel Etingof
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Dan Freed
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Kazhdan"} David Kazhdan
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Morgan"} John Morgan
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Fields+and+Strings"}> Quantum Fields and Strings
                  , A course for mathematicians
                , 2 vols. Amer. Math. Soc. Providence 1999. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ias.edu/qft"}> web version
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Douglas"}> Michael Douglas
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elias+Kiritsis"} Elias Kiritsis
                et. al. (eds.),
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+theory+and+the+real+world"} String theory and the real world
                ,
              %p Les Houches Session LXXXVII 2007
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
                (eds.)
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Mathematical+Foundations+of+Quantum+Field+and+Perturbative+String+Theory"} Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Field and Perturbative String Theory
                ,Proceedings of Symposia in Pure Mathematics,
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/bookstore?fn=20&arg1=pspumseries&ikey=PSPUM-83"} volume 83
                AMS (2011)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Ib%C3%A1%C3%B1ez"}> Luis Ibáñez
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Angel+Uranga"} Angel Uranga
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+Theory+and+Particle+Physics+--+An+Introduction+to+String+Phenomenology"} String Theory and Particle Physics – An Introduction to String Phenomenology
                ,
              %p Cambridge University Press 2012
            %li#West12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+West"}> Peter West
                ,
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Strings+and+Branes"} Introduction to Strings and Branes
                ,
              %p Cambridge University Press 2012
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Moore"}> Gregory Moore
                ,
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Impact+of+D-Branes+on+Mathematics"} The Impact of D-Branes on Mathematics
                ,
              %p
                talk at
                %a{:href => "http://online.kitp.ucsb.edu/online/joefest-c14/"} PolchinskiFest 2014
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.physics.rutgers.edu/~gmoore/JOEFEST-THOUGHTS.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Moore14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregory+Moore"}> Gregory Moore
                ,
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Physical+Mathematics+and+the+Future"} Physical Mathematics and the Future
              %p
                talk at
                %a{:href => "http://physics.princeton.edu/strings2014/"} Strings 2014
                (
                %a{:href => "http://physics.princeton.edu/strings2014/slides/Moore.pdf"}> talk slides
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.physics.rutgers.edu/~gmoore/PhysicalMathematicsAndFuture.pdf"} companion text pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/MooreVisionTalk2014.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#Kane17
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gordon+Kane"}> Gordon Kane
                ,
              %p
                %em> String theory and the real world
                ,
              %p
                Morgan &amp; Claypool, 2017 (
                %a{:href => "http://iopscience.iop.org/book/978-1-6817-4489-6"}> doi:0.1088/978-1-6817-4489-6
                )
              %p
                (on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
                and the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
          %p History:
          %ul
            %li#Schwarz16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"}> John Schwarz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em String Theory in the Twentieth Century
              %a{:href => "http://ymsc.tsinghua.edu.cn:8090/strings/"} Strings 2016
              (
              %a{:href => "http://ymsc.tsinghua.edu.cn:8090/strings/slides/8.1/Schwarz.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            %br/
